\label{prop:ss}
Assume that $\Gamma$ is smooth and $\Gamma^2<0$.
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\alph*)}]
\item We have $H^1(N_{\Gamma/X_0})\cong H^1(\mathcal{O}_{X_0}(\Gamma))=\operatorname{coker} M(Z_0)$. If the points move with velocity $v$, then the class of \eqref{eq:N} is $e(v)=-[(\partial_vM)f]$, and $\Gamma$ lifts to first order along $v$ if and only if $e(v)=0$. We write $\operatorname{ob}(v)=[(\partial_vM)f]$ and call $\operatorname{ob}$ the obstruction map.
\item If $\Gamma^2=-1$, then $N_{\Gamma/\mathcal{X}}$ is semistable if and only if $e(v)\ne0$.
\end{enumerate}

One can ask whether there are other classes which could play the role of $\Theta$. We searched the classes $C=dH-\sum_im_iE_i$ with at most two distinct multiplicities, $d\le200$, $C^2<0$ and expected codimension at most $20$, and we compared the bound given by $s_C(H)$ with Petrakiev's bound $2280/721=3.16227462\ldots$ Only three classes beat it:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
class & $117H-37E$ & $98H-31E$ & $79H-25E$\\
expected codimension & $10$ & $11$ & $11$\\
bound & $3.16227766$ & $3.16227751$ & $3.16227684$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The next Pell class $2280H-721E$ has expected codimension $190$, far above the $12$ moduli of ten points. So going beyond Theorem~A with a single reflection would require a curve much more special than any configuration is expected to carry. For twenty points the analogous search finds no class of expected codimension at most $32$ which beats Petrakiev's bound $720/161$.
